From: kanata-nicola
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 12:08:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] fix(linux): rescan /dev/input when a device node's permissions change

A device node is created by the kernel readable by root only; udev sets its
group (input) and mode a moment later. kanata running as a user in the input
group rescans 200 ms (--wait-device-ms) after the inotify CREATE event; when
udev is slower than that (e.g. right after resume, or several devices behind a
USB hub at once), evdev::enumerate() cannot open the node and silently skips
it, and nothing triggers another rescan until some other device appears. The
keyboard then stays unmapped.

Also watch ATTRIB on /dev/input, so that the permission change made by udev
triggers a rescan as well.

Reproduce: run kanata as a non-root user with --wait-device-ms 0 and replug a
keyboard; without this change it is never grabbed again.
---
--- a/src/oskbd/linux.rs
+++ b/src/oskbd/linux.rs
@@ -765,7 +765,14 @@
 
 fn watch_devinput() -> Result<Inotify, io::Error> {
     let inotify = Inotify::init().expect("Failed to initialize inotify");
-    inotify.watches().add("/dev/input", WatchMask::CREATE)?;
+    // CREATE: a device node appeared. ATTRIB: its owner, mode or ACL changed. A new node is created
+    // by the kernel readable by root only; udev gives it its group (e.g. input) a moment later. When
+    // kanata runs as a user in that group and looks before udev is done, the device cannot be opened
+    // yet and is skipped, and without ATTRIB nothing triggers another look until some other device
+    // appears.
+    inotify
+        .watches()
+        .add("/dev/input", WatchMask::CREATE | WatchMask::ATTRIB)?;
     Ok(inotify)
 }
 
